\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We present a KL distillation framework for training contextual sparsity predictors in SwiGLU-based language models.
The method replaces per-neuron binary classification (BCE) with an output-distribution objective that couples all pruning decisions through the model's forward pass, eliminating the need for auxiliary error compensation networks at inference.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:method:formulation}

Consider a pretrained transformer with $L$ layers, each containing a SwiGLU feed-forward network~\cite{shazeer2020glu}.
For layer $\ell$, the FFN computes:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{y}^{(\ell)} = \bigl(\sigma(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}) \odot \mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)}\bigr) \mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)},
\label{eq:swiglu}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the hidden state after attention ($d{=}4096$), $\sigma$ denotes SiLU, $\mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}, \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_{\text{ff}}}$ and $\mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{ff}} \times d}$ are the gate, up-projection, and down-projection matrices, and $d_{\text{ff}}{=}14{,}336$.

Define the gate activation $\mathbf{g}^{(\ell)} = \sigma(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_g^{(\ell)}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{ff}}}$.
Each element $g_j^{(\ell)}$ determines the contribution of neuron $j$ to the output.
We introduce a binary mask $\mathbf{m}^{(\ell)} \in \{0,1\}^{d_{\text{ff}}}$ that zeros out pruned neurons:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{y}_{\text{sparse}}^{(\ell)} = \bigl(\mathbf{m}^{(\ell)} \odot \mathbf{g}^{(\ell)} \odot \mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)}\bigr) \mathbf{W}_d^{(\ell)}.
\label{eq:sparse_ffn}
\end{align}
Our goal is to learn a mask predictor $f_\theta^{(\ell)}(\mathbf{h}) \to \mathbf{s}^{(\ell)} \in (0,1)^{d_{\text{ff}}}$ that produces per-neuron importance scores from the pre-FFN hidden state $\mathbf{h}$.
At inference, the scores are fed to a global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal} that distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly, retaining the $(1{-}\rho) \times L \times d_{\text{ff}}$ highest-scoring neurons model-wide for a target sparsity ratio $\rho$.


\subsection{Predictor Architecture}
\label{sec:method:predictor}

Each layer $\ell$ has an independent two-layer MLP predictor:
\begin{align}
f_\theta^{(\ell)}(\mathbf{h}) = \text{GELU}(\mathbf{h} \mathbf{W}_1^{(\ell)}) \, \mathbf{W}_2^{(\ell)},
\label{eq:predictor}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{W}_1^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_b}$ and $\mathbf{W}_2^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_b \times d_{\text{ff}}}$ with bottleneck dimension $d_b{=}128$.
The predictor takes the hidden state after attention (before the FFN) and outputs $d_{\text{ff}}$-dimensional logits $\mathbf{s}^{(\ell)}$, from which the mask is derived.

Each predictor has $(4096 \times 128) + (128 \times 14336) = 2.37\text{M}$ parameters.
With 32 independent predictors (one per layer), the total overhead is 75.9M parameters---less than 1\% of the 8B base model.
The predictors operate independently across layers, requiring no cross-layer communication at inference.


\subsection{Gumbel-Sigmoid Relaxation}
\label{sec:method:gumbel}

The hard mask $m_j = \mathbf{1}[s_j > \text{threshold}]$ is non-differentiable, preventing gradient flow from the KL objective through the mask to the predictor parameters.
We apply Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} to produce differentiable soft masks:
\begin{align}
\tilde{m}_j = \sigma_\tau\!\left(\log \frac{s_j}{1 - s_j} + g_1 - g_2\right), \quad g_1, g_2 \sim \text{Gumbel}(0,1),
\label{eq:gumbel}
\end{align}
where $\sigma_\tau(x) = \text{sigmoid}(x / \tau)$ and $\tau$ is a temperature parameter.
The Gumbel noise provides stochastic exploration of mask configurations during training, while the temperature controls sharpness: as $\tau \to 0$, $\tilde{m}_j$ approaches a binary decision.

We anneal $\tau$ from 1.0 to 0.1 over training using a cosine schedule.
At high temperature, the soft mask permits gradient flow through all neurons, enabling broad exploration.
As temperature decreases, the mask concentrates on confident decisions, approximating the hard allocation used at inference.

\paragraph{Comparison with Straight-Through Estimation.}
The straight-through estimator (STE)~\cite{bengio2013ste} applies a hard threshold in the forward pass and passes gradients through unchanged ($\partial m / \partial s = 1$).
In our setting, STE produces discontinuous gradient signals: small parameter changes can flip many neurons simultaneously, causing violent oscillations in the Lagrangian multiplier and failure to converge to the target sparsity.
Gumbel relaxation eliminates this pathology, contributing a 34.5\% improvement (PPL 15.77 $\to$ 11.79 at 50\% sparsity), as we verify empirically in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.


\subsection{KL Divergence Training Objective}
\label{sec:method:kl}

Let $\mathbf{z}_{\text{dense}}$ denote the output logits from a full dense forward pass, and $\mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}}$ the logits from a forward pass with predictor-generated soft masks $\{\tilde{\mathbf{m}}^{(\ell)}\}_\ell$ applied at every layer.
We minimize the forward KL divergence between the dense and sparse output distributions:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} = D_{\text{KL}}(p_{\text{dense}} \,\|\, p_{\text{sparse}}) = \sum_{v} p_{\text{dense}}^{(v)} \log \frac{p_{\text{dense}}^{(v)}}{p_{\text{sparse}}^{(v)}},
\label{eq:kl_loss}
\end{align}
where $p_{\text{dense}} = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{\text{dense}})$ and $p_{\text{sparse}} = \text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}})$, summed over the vocabulary $v$ and averaged over sequence positions.
The base model is frozen; only the predictor parameters receive gradients.

We use forward KL ($D_{\text{KL}}(p_{\text{dense}} \| p_{\text{sparse}})$) rather than reverse KL ($D_{\text{KL}}(p_{\text{sparse}} \| p_{\text{dense}})$).
Forward KL is mean-seeking: it penalizes the sparse model heavily wherever the dense model assigns high probability, forcing the sparse distribution to cover the dense model's primary predictions.
This is appropriate for sparsity prediction, where the goal is to preserve the dense model's top-token behavior rather than explore alternative modes.
No temperature scaling ($T{=}1$) is applied, following the finding that logit-level distillation without temperature is sufficient when the student retains the teacher's architecture~\cite{hinton2015distilling}.


\subsection{Lagrangian Sparsity Constraint}
\label{sec:method:lagrangian}

We enforce a model-level sparsity target $\rho$ via constrained optimization.
Let $\bar{s} = \frac{1}{L} \sum_\ell \frac{1}{d_{\text{ff}}} \sum_j \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}$ denote the average fraction of retained neurons.
The constrained problem is:
\begin{align}
\min_\theta \; \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \bar{s} \leq 1 - \rho.
\label{eq:constrained}
\end{align}
We relax this via the Lagrangian:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} + \lambda \cdot (\bar{s} - (1 - \rho)),
\label{eq:lagrangian}
\end{align}
where $\lambda \geq 0$ is the dual variable, updated by gradient ascent:
\begin{align}
\lambda \leftarrow \lambda + \eta_\lambda \cdot (\bar{s} - (1 - \rho)).
\label{eq:dual_update}
\end{align}
When sparsity undershoots the target ($\bar{s}$ too large), $\lambda$ increases, strengthening the pruning pressure.
When sparsity overshoots, $\lambda$ decreases, allowing the KL objective to recover quality.
This eliminates manual tuning of the quality-sparsity tradeoff weight: the dual variable adapts automatically to balance the two objectives throughout training.


\subsection{Gradient Analysis: BCE vs.\ KL}
\label{sec:method:gradient}

This section formalizes the structural difference between BCE and KL training signals---the core theoretical argument for why KL distillation is better suited to global top-$k$ inference.

\paragraph{BCE gradient structure.}
The standard approach trains the predictor against oracle masks $m_j^* = \mathbf{1}[|g_j| \geq \text{top-}k\text{ threshold}]$ derived from the dense model's activation magnitudes.
The loss decomposes per neuron:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}} = -\frac{1}{d_{\text{ff}}} \sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{ff}}} \bigl[ m_j^* \log \sigma(s_j) + (1 - m_j^*) \log(1 - \sigma(s_j)) \bigr].
\label{eq:bce}
\end{align}
The gradient with respect to predictor logit $s_j$ is:
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}}{\partial s_j} = \sigma(s_j) - m_j^*.
\label{eq:bce_grad}
\end{align}
This gradient depends \emph{only} on neuron $j$'s own prediction and target.
Each neuron is trained independently---there is no inter-neuron coupling in the learning signal.

\paragraph{KL gradient structure.}
Under KL distillation, the gradient for predictor logit $s_j^{(\ell)}$ in layer $\ell$ is:
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}}{\partial s_j^{(\ell)}} = \underbrace{\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}}{\partial \mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}}}}_{\text{(a)}} \cdot \underbrace{\frac{\partial \mathbf{z}_{\text{sparse}}}{\partial \mathbf{h}^{(\ell)}}}_{\text{(b)}} \cdot \underbrace{\frac{\partial \mathbf{h}^{(\ell)}}{\partial \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}}}_{\text{(c)}} \cdot \underbrace{\frac{\partial \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}}{\partial s_j^{(\ell)}}}_{\text{(d)}},
\label{eq:kl_grad}
\end{align}
where each factor has a distinct role:
\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{(a)}] The KL gradient at the output, reflecting how each logit component deviates from the dense distribution.
\item[\textbf{(b)}] The Jacobian through all downstream layers from layer $\ell$'s output to the final logits. This factor couples neuron $j$'s gradient to the state of every other retained neuron in layers $\ell, \ell{+}1, \ldots, L$, because the sparse hidden state propagates through subsequent attention and FFN operations.
\item[\textbf{(c)}] The effect of mask element $\tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)}$ on the layer output: $\partial \mathbf{h}^{(\ell)} / \partial \tilde{m}_j^{(\ell)} = (g_j^{(\ell)} \cdot [\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \mathbf{W}_u^{(\ell)}]_j) \cdot \mathbf{W}_{d,j}^{(\ell)}$, where $\mathbf{W}_{d,j}^{(\ell)}$ is column $j$ of the down-projection. The down-projection linearly mixes all neurons' contributions, so changing $\tilde{m}_j$ affects the full $d$-dimensional hidden state.
\item[\textbf{(d)}] The Gumbel-Sigmoid derivative, providing the differentiable path from logit to mask.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{The structural mismatch.}
The key observation is that factor (b) makes the KL gradient for neuron $j$ depend on \emph{all} other neurons' mask values.
BCE's factorized gradient (Eq.~\ref{eq:bce_grad}) optimizes each neuron's prediction accuracy in isolation, but TEAL inference selects neurons via a global ranking across all layers.
Under global top-$k$, the decision to retain neuron $j$ depends on the scores of all other neurons---a combinatorial interaction that BCE cannot capture.

Concretely, BCE achieves high per-neuron classification accuracy (typically $>$90\% agreement with oracle masks) yet produces poor output quality under TEAL allocation, because it optimizes a proxy (per-neuron accuracy) misaligned with the true objective (output preservation under global budget allocation).
KL directly optimizes the end-to-end objective, with gradients that naturally reflect inter-neuron dependencies through the model's computation graph.
As we show in \S\ref{sec:experiments}, this structural advantage persists even when BCE is given 2.71$\times$ more training compute: the gap is architectural, not computational.


\subsection{Two-Dimensional Taxonomy of Sparsity Prediction Methods}
\label{sec:method:taxonomy}

We organize prior work along two axes: \emph{correction granularity} (how the mask adapts to input) and \emph{training signal coupling} (whether the objective captures inter-neuron dependencies).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Taxonomy of learned sparsity prediction methods. Rows indicate correction granularity; columns indicate training signal coupling. Our method fills a previously unexplored cell.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Decomposed (BCE) & Intermediate (MSE) & Coupled (KL) \\
\midrule
Static & --- & --- & SPON \\
Per-layer & --- & ShadowLLM & --- \\
Per-token & FastForward & --- & \textbf{Ours} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Correction granularity.}
\emph{Static} methods~\cite{xu2026spon} learn a fixed bias vector per layer that does not depend on the input token.
\emph{Per-layer} methods~\cite{akhauri2024shadowllm} condition on a layer-level summary (e.g., the attention output) but share predictions across tokens within a layer.
\emph{Per-token} methods~\cite{gautam2026fastforward,liu2023dejavu} produce a unique mask for each input position.

\paragraph{Training signal coupling.}
\emph{Decomposed} objectives (BCE) treat each neuron independently, optimizing per-element classification against an oracle mask.
\emph{Intermediate} objectives (MSE on hidden states) capture local coupling within a single layer but not end-to-end output effects.
\emph{Coupled} objectives (KL on output distribution) propagate gradients through the full forward pass, reflecting how all mask decisions jointly affect the final distribution.

SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} operates at the intersection of static granularity and coupled training: it distills a per-layer bias correction via KL but cannot adapt to per-input neuron importance.
FastForward~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} achieves per-token granularity but uses BCE training, losing inter-neuron coupling.
Our method combines per-token contextual prediction with coupled KL training (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}), unifying the advantages of both axes: input-dependent mask generation trained with a signal that captures global neuron interactions through the output distribution.

